                heading: 'The four descriptors and how they relate',
                body: 'A single-frequency (pure-tone) sound is fully described by a small set of quantities. FREQUENCY f is how many pressure cycles pass a fixed point per second, measured in hertz (Hz); the human audible range runs roughly 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz, narrowing at the top with age. PERIOD T is the time for one cycle, in seconds, and is simply the reciprocal of frequency: T = 1/f. So a 100 Hz tone has a period of 1/100 = 0.010 s (10 ms), and a 1000 Hz tone has a period of 1 ms. WAVELENGTH λ is the physical distance between successive compressions (one full spatial cycle), in metres. AMPLITUDE is the size of the pressure swing away from ambient — the peak pressure deviation — and it governs loudness, entirely separately from frequency (which governs pitch). Amplitude and frequency are independent: you can make the same 440 Hz note quiet or loud without changing its pitch.',
