                body: 'Waves split into two great families by the direction of oscillation relative to travel. In a LONGITUDINAL wave (sound in air, P-waves in the Earth) the medium oscillates parallel to the propagation direction, producing compressions and rarefactions. In a TRANSVERSE wave (light and all electromagnetic waves, a wave on a string, ripples on water, S-waves in the Earth) the oscillation is perpendicular to travel, producing crests and troughs. Sound in a fluid is purely longitudinal; light is purely transverse. This is not a cosmetic distinction — it has hard physical consequences that judges probe, chief among them polarization.',
