            workedExample: 'A student measures a small speaker outdoors on a grassy field (approximating a free field) with a phone SPL meter. At 1 m they read 80 dB SPL. They predict: at 2 m (one doubling) the level should fall by 6 dB to 74 dB; at 4 m (two doublings) to 68 dB; at 8 m (three doublings) to 62 dB. Their measurements come in at 74, 69, and 63 dB — close, with the small excesses attributable to ground reflections (a real field is not a perfect free field). Then they repeat the experiment inside a tiled bathroom and find the level barely drops from 1 m to 4 m — nothing like −12 dB. Rather than call this a failure, they correctly diagnose it: the hard tiled walls create a strong reverberant field that dominates beyond the critical distance, so the inverse-square law no longer governs. Reporting both the outdoor −6-dB-per-doubling confirmation AND the indoor breakdown, with the reverberant-field explanation, is a far stronger project than either alone.',
            judgeFraming: 'Judges love the inverse-square law because it is quantitative and easy to test, but the discriminating follow-up is "does your −6 dB per doubling hold indoors?" A student who blindly expects inverse-square everywhere gets caught; one who explains that reflections create a reverberant field beyond the critical distance, flattening the level, shows they understand the ASSUMPTION (free field) behind the law rather than treating it as universal. Getting the decibel arithmetic exactly right — −6 dB per doubling, not −3 dB — is itself a competence check judges apply.',
