                body: 'A real spectrum rarely shows every harmonic at equal strength — the source\'s geometry and how it is driven set which harmonics appear and how loud. The textbook case is a cylindrical pipe CLOSED at one end (like a clarinet\'s bore): its boundary conditions permit only the ODD harmonics (f, 3f, 5f…), so the even harmonics are largely absent. That missing-even-harmonic structure is a big part of why a clarinet sounds "hollow" in its low register, and it also makes a closed pipe sound roughly an octave lower than an open pipe of the same length (its fundamental is half the frequency). By contrast a string bowed or plucked at a point suppresses harmonics that have a node there — pluck a guitar string exactly at its midpoint and you knock out the even harmonics (which have a node at the center), changing the timbre. So the SET of harmonics present is itself a fingerprint of the physics, not just decoration.',
