            sections: [
              {
                heading: 'The monochord: one string, one measurable claim',
                body: 'The oldest quantitative result in acoustics predates the word "physics." Sometime in the 6th century BCE, the school associated with Pythagoras of Samos noticed that musical intervals judged "consonant" by ear correspond to simple whole-number ratios of string length. The instrument that made this checkable was the monochord: a single string stretched over a soundboard with a movable bridge. Stop the string at exactly half its length and the shorter segment sounds an octave above the whole string — a length ratio of 2:1. Stop it at two-thirds (3:2) and you get the interval later called the perfect fifth; three-quarters (4:3) gives the perfect fourth. The Pythagoreans elevated this into a metaphysics ("all is number"), but stripped of the mysticism the monochord is a real, repeatable experiment: change one measurable variable (length) and a perceptual quality (consonance) changes with it in a lawful, integer way.',
              },
              {
                heading: 'What Pythagoras got right — and the part he could not know',
                body: 'The genuinely durable discovery is that pitch is governed by ratio, not by absolute length: a string half as long sounds an octave higher whether the whole string is 60 cm or 120 cm. What the ancient account gets subtly wrong is the physical variable. Length maps cleanly to frequency only when tension and mass-per-length are held constant, because for an ideal string the fundamental frequency is f = (1/2L)·√(T/μ), where L is length, T is tension, and μ is mass per unit length. Halving L doubles f — the octave — but that is a fact about frequency, which the Greeks had no way to measure. The full picture (that a musical interval is a ratio of frequencies, not merely of lengths) had to wait roughly two millennia for Galileo Galilei and Marin Mersenne in the early 1600s, who tied pitch directly to vibration rate. Legend also credits Pythagoras with hearing these ratios in the differing weights of blacksmiths\' hammers; that story is physically false — a struck object\'s pitch does not scale simply with hammer weight — and is a useful cautionary tale about tidy origin myths.',
              },
              {
                heading: 'Why the whole-number ratios sound "smooth"',
                body: 'The modern explanation for why simple ratios sound consonant is not mystical: it is about harmonics and beating. A plucked or bowed string vibrates not only at its fundamental f but at integer multiples 2f, 3f, 4f… (the harmonic series). When two notes stand in a simple ratio like 3:2, many of their upper harmonics coincide exactly or fall far apart, so the ear hears few of the slow "beats" and rough interference (roughness) produced when two nearby frequencies clash. Hermann von Helmholtz formalized this roughness account in 1863 (the next lesson). So Pythagoras identified a real correlation between integer ratios and consonance; the mechanism — coinciding harmonics and the absence of beating — is a 19th-century addition, and even today "why exactly do humans prefer consonance" (learned vs. innate) remains partly open.',
              },
            ],
            workedExample: 'A student builds a monochord from a 100 cm guitar string tuned so the open string sounds A2 at 110 Hz. They predict, then test, four bridge positions. Half length (bridge at 50 cm) should give 2×110 = 220 Hz (A3, an octave up) — ratio 2:1. Bridge at 66.7 cm (leaving a 33.3 cm segment is wrong; the sounding segment must be 2/3 of 100 = 66.7 cm) gives frequency (100/66.7)×110 = 165 Hz — a perfect fifth (E3), ratio 3:2. Bridge leaving 75 cm gives (100/75)×110 = 147 Hz — a perfect fourth (D3), ratio 4:3. Measuring the actual pitches with a phone tuner app, they find 220, 165, and 147 Hz within about 1%, confirming that sounding frequency scales as 1/L when tension and string are unchanged — the Pythagorean result made quantitative with a tool the Greeks lacked.',
            judgeFraming: 'A judge probing a pitch-or-consonance project will ask "why do those ratios sound good?" to see whether the student stops at the ancient correlation or can supply the physical mechanism. The revealing answer names harmonics and beating/roughness (Helmholtz), and distinguishes the true variable — frequency ratio — from the thing the Greeks actually measured, length, noting they coincide only at fixed tension and mass. A student who repeats the blacksmith-hammer legend as fact, or claims length alone sets pitch, signals they memorized a story rather than the physics.',
