                    a: 'Telling listeners which is which invites expectation bias; the label, not the sound, can drive the preference.',
                    b: 'Correct — a blind ABX test removes expectation bias and is the standard way to establish whether a difference is actually audible; such tests repeatedly find high-bit-rate/standard-resolution audio hard to distinguish from hi-res.',
                    c: 'File size measures data, not audibility; a bigger file is not proof of an audible difference.',
                    d: 'Spec sheets describe potential capability, not whether a human can hear a difference in practice.',
