            sections: [
              {
                heading: 'From Fourier to Nyquist: the sampling floor',
                body: 'Fourier\'s 1822 insight is that any signal can be decomposed into a sum of sinusoids of different frequencies, amplitudes, and phases. To digitize a signal and later recover its frequency content faithfully, the Nyquist–Shannon sampling theorem sets a hard rule: the sample rate must be at least TWICE the highest frequency present. Human hearing tops out near 20 kHz, so CD audio samples at 44.1 kHz — comfortably above 2×20 kHz = 40 kHz, with margin for the anti-aliasing filter. If a frequency above half the sample rate (the Nyquist frequency) sneaks in, it does not vanish; it ALIASES, folding down and masquerading as a lower frequency. A 30 kHz tone sampled at 44.1 kHz appears as a spurious 44.1 − 30 = 14.1 kHz tone — an artifact that sounds real but is not in the source. This is why an anti-aliasing low-pass filter must remove above-Nyquist content BEFORE sampling, not after.',
              },
              {
                heading: 'The STFT: a moving-window Fourier transform',
                body: 'A plain Fourier transform of a whole recording tells you WHICH frequencies are present but not WHEN — it collapses all time information. Music and speech are non-stationary: the frequencies change moment to moment. The Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT) fixes this by sliding a short analysis window along the signal, computing a Fourier transform (via the FFT algorithm) inside each window, and stacking the results into a time-frequency map. Plotted as intensity-vs-(time, frequency), that map is a SPECTROGRAM — the workhorse display of acoustics, showing a rising pitch as a rising streak and a percussive hit as a vertical smear. The FFT (Cooley–Tukey, 1965) is what made this computationally practical, turning an O(N²) transform into O(N log N).',
              },
              {
                heading: 'The time–frequency tradeoff you cannot escape',
                body: 'The STFT forces an unavoidable compromise governed by an uncertainty relation: a SHORT window localizes events sharply in time but resolves frequency coarsely; a LONG window resolves frequency finely but blurs time. You cannot have both at once — the product of time resolution and frequency resolution is bounded below (Δt·Δf ≳ a constant), the acoustic analogue of the Gabor limit Dennis Gabor formalized in 1946. Practically: to distinguish two close pitches (fine Δf) you need a long window, which then cannot pin down a fast transient; to catch the exact instant of a drum hit (fine Δt) you need a short window, which then smears nearby frequencies together. Choosing the window length is therefore the central judgment call in making a spectrogram, and there is no universally correct choice — it depends on whether your question is about pitch or timing.',
              },
            ],
            workedExample: 'A student records ultrasonic bat calls and wants to analyze them, but their audio interface samples at 44.1 kHz. Bat calls sweep from ~20 kHz to ~80 kHz. By Nyquist, 44.1 kHz can only faithfully capture up to 22.05 kHz — everything above aliases. An 80 kHz component folds to |80 − 2·44.1| ... more simply it wraps repeatedly and reappears as a false low-frequency tone, so the spectrogram shows streaks that are pure artifact. The fix: use a 192 kHz (or higher) sample rate so Nyquist (96 kHz) exceeds the 80 kHz upper edge. Then, choosing a window: the student wants to see the rapid frequency sweep of each chirp, so they pick a SHORT window for good time resolution, accepting coarser frequency bins — a deliberate trade because the chirp\'s timing, not fine pitch, is the target.',
            judgeFraming: 'A judge probes the STFT to test whether a student who shows a spectrogram understands its limits or just pasted a colorful plot. The two discriminating facts are Nyquist (sample rate ≥ 2× highest frequency, or you get aliasing) and the time–frequency tradeoff (you cannot have sharp time AND sharp frequency simultaneously). A student who can say "I widened my window to separate the two harmonics, which cost me time resolution" has made an informed choice; one who cannot explain why their spectrogram is blurry in one axis has not understood the tool they are using.',
