              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'Millington-Sette: take the log of each surface, then sum',
                  body: 'Both Sabine and Eyring collapse a room to a single average absorption coefficient. Millington (1932) and Sette (1933), publishing back-to-back in JASA, argued this is wrong when surfaces differ strongly. Their fix: instead of averaging the αᵢ and then taking one logarithm, take the logarithm of each surface separately and sum. The Millington-Sette absorption is A = −Σ Sᵢ·ln(1−αᵢ), so RT60 = 0.161·V / (−Σ Sᵢ·ln(1−αᵢ)). For a room with uniform α this is identical to Eyring, but when one wall is much more absorptive than the rest, the per-surface logs weight the strong absorber more honestly. The formula has a famous quirk: if ANY single surface has αᵢ = 1 (a perfect absorber, e.g. an open window), its −ln(1−1) = +∞ term drives the total absorption to infinity and RT60 to zero — which is arguably correct for a small perfect absorber only in the diffuse-field idealisation, and is often cited as the formula\'s weakness because a modest open window does not really kill all reverberation.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Fitzroy: absorption is not diffuse — average by axis',
                  body: 'Dariel Fitzroy (1959, JASA, "Reverberation formula which seems to be more accurate with nonuniform distribution of absorption") attacked a different assumption. In many real rooms — a classroom with an absorptive ceiling but hard floor and walls — sound does NOT reach a diffuse field; energy decays at different rates along the three axes (floor-ceiling vs the two horizontal pairs). Fitzroy proposed computing an Eyring-style reverberation time SEPARATELY for each pair of opposite surfaces and combining them as an area-weighted average of the three axis reverberation times. Concretely, the room is split into three sets of parallel surfaces (x, y, z), each with its own average absorption, and RT is a weighted blend. Fitzroy\'s formula is empirical and clumsy but was a real advance for rooms with absorption concentrated on one pair of surfaces — exactly the classroom-with-treated-ceiling case where Sabine and Eyring systematically under-predict because they smear the concentrated absorption over the whole room.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'A genuine methods debate, not a settled hierarchy',
                  body: 'There is no single "winner." Sabine remains the ISO/ASTM standard for laboratory absorption measurement precisely because its simplicity makes coefficient values comparable across labs (the published αSabine you look up assumes the Sabine model). Eyring is preferred for prediction in uniformly absorptive rooms. Millington-Sette is theoretically appealing for strongly mixed surfaces but its open-window singularity limits practical trust. Fitzroy targets non-diffuse rooms with one-sided absorption but is empirical and can itself mispredict when absorption is actually well distributed. Modern practice increasingly bypasses all of them with ray-tracing or image-source simulation for complex rooms, and ISO 3382 measures RT directly rather than predicting it. The lesson for a student: these are competing MODELS with different failure modes, and citing which one you used — and why — is itself a mark of rigor.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'A classroom V = 200 m³ has floor+walls of S = 160 m² at α = 0.05 and an acoustically treated ceiling of S = 40 m² at α = 0.85. Eyring lumps everything: area-weighted ᾱ = (160×0.05 + 40×0.85)/200 = (8 + 34)/200 = 0.21, so RT60 = 0.161 × 200 / (200 × (−ln(1−0.21))) = 32.2 / (200 × 0.2357) = 32.2/47.1 = 0.68 s. Millington-Sette keeps surfaces separate: A = −[160·ln(0.95) + 40·ln(0.15)] = −[160×(−0.0513) + 40×(−1.897)] = 8.21 + 75.9 = 84.1 sabins, so RT60 = 0.161 × 200 / 84.1 = 32.2/84.1 = 0.38 s. The two differ by nearly a factor of two because the ceiling\'s strong absorption is weighted far more heavily once you log each surface before summing — a concrete illustration of why the averaging order matters when surfaces are wildly different.',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges probe the rival formulas to see whether a student treats "reverberation time" as one equation or as a modeling choice. Naming Millington-Sette (log each surface, then sum — with the open-window singularity) and Fitzroy (axis-by-axis averaging for one-sided absorption) accurately, and explaining that Sabine survives as the measurement standard while the others are prediction refinements, demonstrates the student understands the field as a live debate. That is exactly the depth a judge rewards over "I used the Sabine formula because it was in the textbook."',
