              workedExample: 'A team must specify treatment for a 300 m³ multipurpose room, S = 280 m². Draft design: hard floor and walls at α = 0.06 over 240 m², plus 40 m² of ceiling panels at α = 0.90. Step 1 — mean absorption: ᾱ = (240×0.06 + 40×0.90)/280 = (14.4 + 36)/280 = 0.18. Step 2 — uniformity: absorption is highly concentrated on the ceiling (one pair of surfaces), so the diffuse-field assumption is shaky. Applying the rule: ᾱ is near the 0.2 boundary and distribution is strongly one-sided, so the team computes both Eyring (single average) and Fitzroy-style axis reasoning, and reports that the single-average Eyring value, RT60 = 0.161×300/(280×(−ln(0.82))) = 48.3/(280×0.198) = 48.3/55.5 = 0.87 s, likely UNDER-states reality because concentrating absorption on one axis leaves the horizontal axis under-damped — so they add wall treatment to distribute absorption and bring the room back toward the diffuse regime where their prediction is trustworthy.',
              judgeFraming: 'This is the capstone judges care about most: not "can you plug into Sabine" but "can you justify WHICH formula you used and predict its error." A student who says "ᾱ was 0.18 and uniform, so Sabine and Eyring agree and I reported Sabine; but if the ceiling had carried all the absorption I\'d have distrusted the diffuse-field assumption and checked Fitzroy" has demonstrated exactly the methods judgment a research fair is meant to reward — choosing a tool with awareness of its domain, rather than defaulting to the one equation they memorised.',
