              judgeFraming: 'Judges ask about room ratios to see whether a student knows the difference between DISTRIBUTING modes and REMOVING them — a distinction that trips up even hobbyists. A strong answer cites that a cube is the worst case because its three axial series coincide, names the Bolt (1946) origin of ratio criteria, and — crucially — concedes the honest limit: a good ratio helps but treatment and placement still matter, and DSP/bass traps can rescue a bad ratio. A student who claims a golden-ratio room "has no modes" has misunderstood; a student who says "the ratio spreads the modes evenly but I still trapped the corners" has it exactly right.',
