              readingMinutes: 5,
              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'Flutter echo: a repeating slap between parallel walls',
                  body: 'Not every room problem is a low-frequency mode. FLUTTER ECHO is a distinct, mid/high-frequency phenomenon: a rapid, repetitive "buzzy" or "boing" ringing you hear when a transient sound bounces back and forth between two hard, parallel, untreated surfaces. Clap your hands in an empty tiled corridor and the metallic zing you hear is flutter echo. Its repetition rate is f = c/(2d), where d is the distance between the parallel walls — the same c/(2d) that gives the axial mode fundamental, because flutter is really the time-domain manifestation of a strongly excited axial mode series in the region where modes are dense enough to be heard as a periodic click train rather than a single tone. A 3.4 m spacing gives a flutter repetition of 343/(2·3.4) ≈ 50 Hz — you perceive a pitched buzz around there. The fix is simple and satisfying: break the parallelism (splay one wall a few degrees), or absorb/diffuse ONE of the two surfaces so the back-and-forth can no longer sustain.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Comb filtering: interference between a direct sound and its reflection',
                  body: 'COMB FILTERING is the frequency-domain cousin of the early-reflection problem. When the direct sound from a source and a single strong early reflection (off a nearby desk, wall, or console) arrive at your ear with a time delay Δt, they interfere: frequencies where the path difference is a whole number of wavelengths add (constructive), and frequencies where it is an odd half-wavelength cancel (destructive). The result is a series of evenly spaced peaks and deep notches across the spectrum — a pattern that looks like the teeth of a comb, hence the name. The notches sit at f = (2n–1)/(2·Δt) for n = 1,2,3..., so the first notch is at 1/(2·Δt) and they repeat every 1/Δt. A reflection delayed by 1 ms puts the first notch at 500 Hz with notches spaced 1000 Hz apart; a 0.5 ms delay pushes the whole comb up to a 1 kHz first notch. Comb filtering is why a mix can sound "phasey" or "hollow" from certain seats, and why the first reflection points on a control-room wall are treated (absorbed or diffused) to weaken the reflection relative to the direct sound.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Early reflections, the Haas effect, and why timing matters',
                  body: 'Whether an early reflection helps or hurts depends on its DELAY and LEVEL relative to the direct sound. Reflections arriving within about 1 ms combine to comb-filter the tone (a coloration problem). Reflections arriving roughly 5–35 ms after the direct sound fall in the range of the Haas (precedence) effect — first characterized by Helmut Haas in his 1949–1951 doctoral work — where the ear fuses the reflection with the direct sound and localizes toward the FIRST arrival, so a modest early reflection can add useful loudness and spaciousness without being heard as a separate echo, provided it stays below a level threshold (Haas found the reflection could be up to ~10 dB louder before it began to shift localization). Reflections beyond ~50 ms in speech (the "initial time delay gap" and the boundary of the C50/D50 clarity metrics) start to be heard as distinct echoes and degrade intelligibility. So the design task is not "kill all reflections" but manage their timing and level: suppress the sub-millisecond comb-filtering reflections at the mix position, exploit early reflections for envelopment, and prevent late discrete echoes.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'An engineer notices a hollow, phasey coloration at the mix seat. A reflection is coming off the mixing desk surface: the direct path from monitor to ears is 1.20 m, and the reflected path (down to the desk and back up) is 1.54 m, a path difference of 0.34 m. The extra delay is Δt = 0.34 / 343 = 0.99 ms ≈ 1.0 ms. Comb notches fall at f = (2n–1)/(2·Δt): first notch 1/(2·0.001) = 500 Hz, then 1500, 2500 Hz, spaced 1/Δt = 1000 Hz apart — exactly the "hollow" region. Separately, the engineer claps and hears a 45 Hz-ish buzz between the two side walls 3.8 m apart: flutter at c/(2d) = 343/7.6 ≈ 45 Hz. Two different problems, two different fixes: an absorptive pad on the desk bridge weakens the comb-filtering reflection, and splaying or diffusing one side wall kills the flutter. Note both share the c/(2d) family of arithmetic but live in different domains — comb filtering in the frequency response, flutter in the time-domain echo train.',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges use flutter echo and comb filtering to test whether a student separates the low-frequency MODAL problem (Lessons 1–4) from the mid/high-frequency EARLY-REFLECTION problem — a distinction amateurs collapse into "the room sounds bad." A strong student computes a comb notch from a measured path difference (f = 1/(2·Δt)), explains that flutter is a parallel-wall phenomenon fixed by breaking parallelism or absorbing one surface, and invokes the Haas/precedence window to argue that not all early reflections are harmful. Naming Haas (1949–51) accurately and giving a real delay-to-frequency calculation is the mark of genuine understanding rather than jargon.',
