                  body: 'Because intervals multiply (stacking a fifth on a fifth means multiplying 3/2 by 3/2), comparing them is awkward in raw ratios. The cent, proposed by the acoustician Alexander Ellis in his 1885 appendix to his English translation of Helmholtz\'s "On the Sensations of Tone," fixes this by taking a logarithm. One octave is defined as exactly 1200 cents, so each of the twelve equal-tempered semitones is 100 cents. The conversion for any frequency ratio r is: cents = 1200 × log2(r), equivalently 1200 × ln(r) / ln(2), or 3986.31 × log10(r). Cents turn multiplication into addition — to stack two intervals you simply add their cent values — which is exactly why every modern discussion of tuning error is quoted in cents rather than in raw ratios or in hertz.',
