              workedExample: 'Show that the D–A fifth in a C-major just scale is a wolf. In C-major just intonation, D is tuned as the major whole tone above the tonic, 9/8, and A is the just major sixth, 5/3. The interval from D up to A is the ratio (5/3) ÷ (9/8) = (5 × 8)/(3 × 9) = 40/27 = 1.4815. In cents: 1200 × log2(40/27) = 1200 × log2(1.4815) = 680.4 cents. A pure fifth is 702.0 cents, so this D–A is 702.0 − 680.4 = 21.5 cents flat — exactly one syntonic comma (81/80). That 21.5-cent shortfall is far above the ~5-cent JND, so a sustained D–A dyad beats obviously: the near-coincident 3rd-harmonic-of-D and 2nd-harmonic-of-A differ by enough hertz to produce an audible warble. This single wolf fifth is the concrete price of tuning everything else pure.',
