              judgeFraming: 'Judges ask "what did quarter-comma meantone give up to get pure thirds, and where did the error go?" to check that the student understands temperament as a conservation problem, not a free lunch. The strong answer: meantone narrows four fifths by a quarter comma each to make the third pure (~696.6-cent fifths), and the accumulated error is dumped into one ~737.6-cent wolf fifth, making some keys unusable — which is precisely why well temperaments were invented to spread the comma so all 24 keys work, and why the "Bach = equal temperament" claim is a contested oversimplification.',
