              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'What Chladni actually did',
                  body: 'In 1787 the German physicist and musician Ernst Chladni published Entdeckungen über die Theorie des Klanges ("Discoveries in the Theory of Sound"). His famous demonstration: scatter fine sand on a flat metal plate clamped at its center, then draw a violin bow across the plate\'s edge. The bow drives the plate into one of its natural modes of vibration. Sand bounces away from the moving (antinode) regions and collects along the lines that stay still — the nodal lines — tracing out a symmetric geometric pattern now called a Chladni figure. Bow at different points, or touch a fingertip to force a node at a chosen spot, and the plate jumps to a different mode with a different, higher pattern and a higher pitch. Chladni catalogued dozens of these patterns for square and circular plates, giving the first systematic, repeatable, visible record of two-dimensional standing waves.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Why the sand collects on nodal lines',
                  body: 'A vibrating plate is a two-dimensional standing-wave system, the plate analogue of a string\'s standing waves. At an antinode the surface oscillates with large amplitude; at a node it is (ideally) motionless. Sand grains sitting on a moving region are thrown upward each cycle and skitter away — a chaotic random walk — until they land on a node, where the surface does not move and the grains are no longer ejected, so they accumulate there. The pattern you see is therefore a direct map of the plate\'s nodal lines for whichever mode is excited. Each mode has its own frequency; higher modes have more nodal lines packed closer together and sound at higher frequencies, so the visible geometry and the audible pitch rise together. (A subtlety Chladni could not see: very fine powder, like lycopodium spores, instead collects at the antinodes because it is carried by the acoustic streaming of the air rather than bouncing on the plate — a distinction Michael Faraday investigated in 1831.)',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Why this founded experimental acoustics',
                  body: 'Before Chladni, sound was studied mostly through the vibrating string (Pythagoras\'s monochord, Mersenne\'s and Sauveur\'s work on frequency in the 1600s–1700s) and open questions about air columns. Chladni made an invisible vibration visible and, crucially, reproducible — anyone with a plate, a bow, and sand could recreate the exact figures, so the phenomenon became a shared object of study rather than a fleeting sound. He toured Europe demonstrating the plates; Napoleon reportedly funded a French translation and offered a prize (won by Sophie Germain in 1816) for a mathematical theory of the vibrating plate, which pushed the development of plate-bending elasticity theory. Chladni also measured the speed of sound in different gases and is separately regarded as a founder of meteoritics. His plate figures directly seeded modern experimental modal analysis — the engineering discipline of measuring the vibration mode shapes of real structures, from violin bodies to car chassis to aircraft panels.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'A student clamps a 20 cm square steel plate at its center and bows the midpoint of one edge, producing an "X"-shaped pattern of sand: two diagonal nodal lines crossing at the center. They record the pitch with a phone tuner at 340 Hz. They then lightly touch a fingertip to the midpoint of one edge (forcing a node there) and re-bow a corner; the sand rearranges into a finer grid pattern with more nodal lines and the tuner now reads about 680 Hz — roughly double, consistent with jumping to a higher mode. The student\'s key insight, written up correctly: the sand is not "showing where the plate vibrates" but the opposite — it collects on the nodal lines, the parts that stay still — and each distinct figure is one resonant mode of the plate, with higher, more intricate figures corresponding to higher frequencies.',
              judgeFraming: 'A judge who sees a Chladni-plate project will immediately ask "where does the sand end up, and why?" — because the single most common student error is to say the sand shows where the plate is vibrating. It shows the exact opposite: the nodes, the still lines. A student who says "the grains get bounced off the antinodes and pile up on the nodal lines, so each figure is a map of one vibration mode" has understood standing waves in 2D, not just reproduced a pretty picture. Judges also reward knowing that changing where you bow or damp selects a different mode, and that Chladni figures are the historical root of modern modal analysis.',
