              readingMinutes: 5,
              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'Every noise problem is a chain of three links',
                  body: 'Noise control engineering rests on one organizing model: every noise problem is a system of a SOURCE that radiates acoustic energy, a PATH along which that energy travels, and a RECEIVER (usually a person, sometimes a microphone or an instrument) where the energy does its unwanted work. This decomposition is not academic tidiness — it is the decision tree. You can only reduce a receiver\'s exposure by intervening at one of these three links, and the three interventions have wildly different cost, effectiveness, and feasibility. The framework appears in essentially every standard text (Beranek & Vér\'s "Noise and Vibration Control Engineering," Bies, Hansen & Howard\'s "Engineering Noise Control," and the U.S. OSHA/NIOSH hierarchy of controls) precisely because it forces the first and most important question: which link am I attacking, and is it the right one?',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Attack the source first — the engineering hierarchy',
                  body: 'The controls have a well-established order of preference. Attacking the SOURCE (quieter machinery, balanced fans, lower-speed operation, vibration isolation of the exciting force, replacing metal-on-metal impacts with damped materials) is almost always the most effective and most durable fix because it removes the energy before it is ever radiated — every downstream path and receiver benefits at once. Attacking the PATH (barriers, enclosures, silencers/mufflers, absorptive room treatment, distance) is the classic domain of "soundproofing" and is where most retrofit work lives, because you often cannot change a source you do not own. Attacking the RECEIVER (hearing protectors, moving people, limiting exposure time, administrative rotation) is the fallback — cheapest per unit but least reliable, because it depends on human compliance and protects only the people wearing the protection, not bystanders. NIOSH is explicit that receiver-side hearing protection is the LAST resort, not the first.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'The framework tells you where measurement and blame belong',
                  body: 'The model also disciplines diagnosis. A receiver complaint ("it is too loud at my desk") is not yet a source or a path statement. Good practice is to characterize each link: measure the source\'s sound power (a property of the machine, in watts / dB re 1 pW), then account for how the path — distance, walls, reflections, ducts — transforms that power into the sound pressure (dB re 20 µPa) the receiver actually experiences. Confusing sound power (what the source emits, independent of the room) with sound pressure (what a location experiences, dependent on the room) is the single most common conceptual error in the field, and the source-path-receiver split is what keeps them separate: power is a source property, pressure is a receiver-location property, and the path is the transfer function between them.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'A workshop has a bandsaw that reads 92 dB(A) at the operator\'s ear. The team lists one option per link and estimates each. SOURCE: mount the saw on vibration isolators and add a damped blade guard — removes structure-borne radiation from the panels, estimated 6 dB, and it helps everyone in the room. PATH: build a partial absorptive enclosure open on the feed side — the open side leaks, so a partial enclosure realistically yields ~8–10 dB, not the 25+ dB a sealed enclosure could give. RECEIVER: issue earmuffs rated NRR 25 — on paper 25 dB, but the rating is not a straight subtraction from an A-weighted level (the NRR carries a 7 dB C-to-A adjustment before any real-world derate) and NRRs are commonly halved in practice, so the effective figure lands around ~12 dB and protects only the wearer. The framework makes the tradeoff legible: the source fix is modest but universal and permanent, the enclosure is large but compromised by the required opening, and the earmuffs are cheap but protect one person and depend on them wearing them all shift.',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges ask "where in the source-path-receiver chain did you intervene, and why there?" because it instantly separates a student who bolted on a random fix from one who diagnosed the system. The tell of understanding is that the student can name the link they chose, name the links they rejected, and say WHY — usually invoking the hierarchy (source > path > receiver) or a feasibility constraint ("we could not modify the motor, so we treated the path"). A student who says "we added foam" without saying which link foam attacks, or who reaches for earplugs before considering the source, has not internalized the framework.',
