              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'Why the mass law is not the whole story',
                  body: 'The airborne sound-insulation of a single panel is idealized by the mass law: transmission loss rises about 6 dB for every doubling of surface mass (mass per unit area, kg/m^2) and about 6 dB for every doubling of frequency. A rigid, limp, infinitely damped panel would follow that line forever. Real panels do not, because a real panel is a plate that bends, and a plate has its own resonances and its own bending waves. Two mechanical effects punch holes in the mass-law line: at low frequency the panel-and-cavity or panel-and-stiffness resonances let sound through, and at high frequency the coincidence effect (the next section) opens a broad dip. In both regions the only thing standing between you and a leak is damping — the panel\'s ability to turn vibrational energy into heat instead of re-radiating it as sound. Damping is the lever the mass law cannot buy: you can add mass to shift the whole curve up, but only damping fills in the resonant dips.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'The loss factor eta, and where the energy goes',
                  body: 'Damping is quantified by the loss factor, written as the Greek letter eta. Physically eta is the fraction of stored vibrational energy dissipated per radian of oscillation; equivalently it is the reciprocal of the quality factor Q (eta = 1/Q) and is twice the damping ratio zeta (eta = 2·zeta) for light damping. At a resonance, the amplitude of a driven panel is limited only by damping, so the height of the transmission-loss dip at resonance is set almost entirely by eta: doubling the loss factor raises the transmission loss at that dip by roughly 3 dB (10·log10 of the ratio of loss factors). A bare sheet of steel or glass has a tiny intrinsic loss factor, on the order of eta ≈ 0.001 to 0.005 — it "rings" when struck. Plasterboard/gypsum sits higher, around eta ≈ 0.01 to 0.03. Adding a damping treatment (a viscoelastic layer) can push an effective panel loss factor to eta ≈ 0.1 or more, which is where the resonant dips genuinely fill in. The energy dissipated per cycle scales with eta, and that mechanical energy leaves the panel as heat, not as re-radiated sound.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Coincidence and the critical frequency',
                  body: 'The worst offender in a single panel is the coincidence dip. A bending wave travels along a plate at a speed that increases with frequency (bending-wave speed rises as the square root of frequency). An airborne sound wave hitting the panel at an angle projects a "trace" wavelength along the surface. At one special frequency and angle the airborne trace wavelength exactly matches the panel\'s free bending wavelength — the wave and the plate move in lockstep, coupling becomes extremely efficient, and transmission loss collapses. The lowest frequency at which this can happen (grazing incidence) is the critical frequency, fc. It scales inversely with panel thickness: thicker, stiffer panels have a LOWER critical frequency. That is the counterintuitive engineering fact — a thick, stiff wall drops its coincidence dip down into the speech range (a 13 mm gypsum board has fc near 2.5–3 kHz; a thick concrete slab can have fc down around 100–200 Hz). Above fc the mass law resumes, but the depth of the coincidence dip itself is governed by the loss factor: a well-damped panel barely dips, an undamped glass pane dives many dB right in the frequency band your ear cares about most.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'A student measures the transmission loss of a single 4 mm glass pane and finds a sharp dip of about 8 dB near 3 kHz, exactly where speech consonants live. The intrinsic loss factor of glass is roughly eta ≈ 0.002 (Q ≈ 500). They bond a thin polymer damping film to one face, raising the effective loss factor of the treated pane to about eta ≈ 0.02 — a tenfold increase. Because the dip depth at resonance improves by about 10·log10(eta_new / eta_old) = 10·log10(0.02 / 0.002) = 10 dB, the coincidence dip largely fills in and the treated pane\'s transmission loss near 3 kHz recovers by roughly 8–10 dB. Crucially, adding mass (a thicker pane) would have moved the dip to a DIFFERENT frequency but not removed it — only the damping film flattened the dip in place.',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges probe damping because "just add mass" is the tell of a student who memorized the mass law and stopped. The give-away of real understanding is naming the loss factor eta and explaining that resonant dips (low-frequency panel resonance and the coincidence dip) are limited by damping, not mass. A student who can say "my coincidence dip is at the critical frequency, and I filled it in by raising eta with a viscoelastic layer" has understood that mass shifts the curve while damping fills its holes — two independent levers, not one.',
