              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'Airborne vs structure-borne: two different problems',
                  body: 'STC and Rw rate AIRBORNE sound — a source radiates into the air, the air drives the partition, and the partition re-radiates on the far side. Footsteps, dropped objects, and dragged chairs are STRUCTURE-BORNE (impact) sound: the source injects vibration directly into the floor structure, which then radiates into the room below without ever passing through air on the source side. These are physically distinct transmission paths, so a floor can be excellent at airborne isolation and terrible at impact isolation, or vice versa. That is why impact needs its OWN rating: Impact Insulation Class (IIC) in North America (ASTM E492 lab / E1007 field) and the weighted normalized impact sound pressure level Ln,w in ISO 717-2. IIC is a "higher is better" rating like STC; Ln,w is a "lower is better" level, so the two run in opposite directions and must not be confused.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'The standardized tapping machine',
                  body: 'You cannot rate impact isolation with a real footstep — it is not reproducible. The standard source is the ISO/ASTM tapping machine: a device with five steel hammers (each ~500 g) that fall in sequence from a fixed height, delivering ten impacts per second onto the floor under test. A calibrated microphone array in the room below measures the resulting one-third-octave sound pressure levels (100 Hz to 3150 Hz for IIC). Because the excitation is fixed and standardized, differences in the measured levels reflect the floor/ceiling assembly alone. The tapping machine\'s hard steel hammers are quite good at exciting the mid and high frequencies, which is one root of a long-running critique: the machine over-represents the "click" of hard-heeled shoes and under-represents the low-frequency "thud" of a bare-foot or soft-soled footfall.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'The tapping-machine controversy and heavy/soft-impact sources',
                  body: 'The core debate is that the standard tapping machine does not correlate well with subjective annoyance from real footsteps on many modern floors — especially lightweight wood-joist assemblies, where the dominant complaint is low-frequency thud. Japanese and Korean research and standards responded with a "heavy/soft impact source" — the bang machine (a swinging rubber tire) and later a standardized rubber ball dropped from 1 m — which puts far more energy into the 63 Hz region and correlates better with real footfall on those floors. ISO 717-2 added a low-frequency spectrum adaptation term CI (and CI,50–2500) to extend impact rating toward the frequencies that actually bother people, mirroring what Ctr did for airborne traffic noise. The practical upshot for a student: a floor with a great IIC can still generate footfall complaints if it fails at low frequency, because the tapping machine that produced the IIC under-tests exactly the band that matters for a soft, heavy footfall. A soft resilient underlayment or carpet dramatically improves IIC (it kills the high-frequency click the machine loves) while doing little for the low-frequency thud — a mismatch that has fooled many real projects.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'A student tests a bare concrete slab and a slab with a carpet-plus-pad finish using a borrowed tapping machine, reading the normalized impact level in each one-third-octave band and fitting the IIC contour (ASTM E989, analogous to the STC fit but for impact). The bare slab measures IIC 28 — high impact levels everywhere, dominated by the sharp high-frequency click of steel-on-concrete. Adding carpet and pad, they re-measure IIC 78, a 50-point jump, because the soft finish crushes the high-frequency click the tapping machine emphasizes. But when the student also drops a standardized rubber ball and looks at the 63 Hz band, the improvement is only a few dB: the carpet barely touches the low-frequency energy. They conclude honestly that the IIC leapt from 28 to 78 mostly because the tapping machine rewards killing the click, and that a downstairs neighbour with heavy footfalls would notice far less improvement than the 50-point IIC gain suggests.',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges ask "why do you need a separate impact rating, and why the tapping machine?" to check whether a student understands that footfall is structure-borne, not airborne, and cannot be predicted from STC. The tell of depth is knowing the tapping machine has hard steel hammers that over-represent high-frequency click and under-represent low-frequency thud — and that this is why a carpet can add 50 IIC points yet still leave footfall complaints, which motivated the rubber-ball heavy-impact source and the CI low-frequency term.',
