              readingMinutes: 6,
              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'The hair bundle: a staircase of stereocilia',
                  body: 'On top of each hair cell sits a hair bundle — not true hairs but stereocilia, stiff actin-filled rods arranged in rows of increasing height, like a staircase. The tallest row leans against (or, for outer hair cells, is embedded in) the tectorial membrane. When the basilar membrane moves and shear develops, the whole bundle is deflected sideways. Crucially, deflection is directional: pushing the bundle toward its tall edge opens transduction channels (excitation), pushing toward the short edge closes them below their resting level (inhibition), and pushing perpendicular does little. The bundle is exquisitely sensitive — at threshold, the tips move only a fraction of a nanometer, less than the diameter of an atom, which is part of why the ear is such an extraordinary detector.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Tip links: the molecular strings that gate the channels',
                  body: 'Running from the tip of each shorter stereocilium up to the side of its taller neighbor is a fine filament called the tip link, made of two proteins, cadherin-23 and protocadherin-15 (established through work by groups including Ulrich Müller\'s and others in the 2000s). The tip link is the mechanical trigger: when the bundle deflects toward the tall edge, the tip links are stretched, and that tension pulls open the mechanotransduction (MET) ion channels — a directly mechanically gated channel, with no chemical intermediate and no delay for a second messenger. This direct gating is why hearing is fast enough to follow sound: the channel opens within microseconds of the mechanical stimulus, far faster than the millisecond-scale chemical cascades of vision. If tip links are broken (for example by very loud sound or by briefly removing calcium), transduction stops until they regrow over hours.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Adaptation and the gating-spring model',
                  body: 'The system does not just open and stay open. Hair cells adapt: during a sustained deflection the channels partly re-close, resetting sensitivity so the cell can respond to further changes rather than saturating. A molecular motor built on myosin is thought to climb and slip along the actin core to adjust tip-link tension, and calcium entering through the open channel promotes re-closure. This whole picture is captured by the "gating-spring" model, developed by James Hudspeth, David Corey, and colleagues from the late 1970s onward: the tip link behaves like an elastic spring in series with the channel gate, so bundle displacement is converted into gating force. It is worth naming the caveat honestly: while the gating-spring framework and the tip-link identity are well established, the exact identity of the pore-forming MET channel protein (TMC1 is the leading candidate, from Jeffrey Holt and others) was debated for years and only relatively recently pinned down — a live example of how mechanistic detail in this field is still being settled.',
                },
              ],
