              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'You cannot report a level you did not calibrate',
                  body: 'Every threshold and every loudness number is only as trustworthy as the calibration of the sound delivered to the ear. The consumer chain — laptop volume slider → OS mixer → headphone amp → transducer — has an unknown, nonlinear gain, so a "volume = 50%" setting is not a physical level and must never be reported as one. Real psychoacoustics calibrates OUTPUT: for headphones/insert earphones, the transducer is coupled to an ARTIFICIAL EAR or 2-cc coupler containing a calibrated measurement microphone and a sound level meter, so a given digital signal is mapped to a known dB SPL at the eardrum reference point; the whole meter chain is itself checked against a pistonphone or acoustic calibrator (a device that emits a known level, e.g. 94 dB SPL at 1 kHz = 1 Pa). Frequency matters: a flat digital signal does NOT produce a flat SPL, because the transducer and coupler have their own frequency response, so calibration must be done PER frequency you test. A student project that cannot state "X dB SPL at the ear" for its stimuli has, strictly, no level axis at all.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Headphones vs free field: dB HL, the MAF/MAP gap, and diffuse-field correction',
                  body: 'Where you measure "the level" changes the number. FREE-FIELD listening (a loudspeaker in an anechoic space, listener facing it) is quantified by the minimum audible FIELD (MAF) — the SPL measured at the position the listener\'s head WOULD occupy, with the listener absent. HEADPHONE listening is quantified by the minimum audible PRESSURE (MAP) — the SPL right at the eardrum. MAF and MAP differ (the classic ~6–10 dB "missing 6 dB" discrepancy, partly from head/pinna diffraction and binaural summation in free field), which is why free-field and headphone thresholds are not directly interchangeable. Clinical audiometry sidesteps this by using dB HL with transducer-specific reference equivalent threshold sound pressure levels (RETSPLs, per ISO 389) so that 0 dB HL means normal for THAT transducer. For a fair project the lesson is blunt: state your transducer and delivery mode, and do not compare a headphone threshold in one condition to a speaker threshold in another as if the dB numbers were on the same scale.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'The threats that quietly wreck a listening study',
                  body: 'Beyond calibration, several validity threats routinely sink student projects. (1) AMBIENT NOISE — a quiet bedroom is typically 30–40 dB(A), which MASKS low-level tones and inflates thresholds; genuine threshold work needs a sound-treated booth meeting the ANSI S3.1 maximum permissible ambient noise levels. (2) The DOUBLE-BLIND / EXPECTANCY problem — if the experimenter knows which interval holds the signal and gives an unconscious cue (a glance, a pause), or the listener knows the condition, results are biased; catch trials, randomized interval assignment, and a blinded operator guard against this. (3) TRAINING and ORDER effects — performance improves over the first runs (perceptual learning) and drifts with fatigue, so condition order must be counterbalanced and practice trials excluded. (4) The FREQUENCY-RESPONSE illusion — if cheap earbuds boost the bass, a "low-frequency sensitivity" finding may be the earbuds, not the ears. (5) Uncontrolled BINAURAL vs monaural presentation — binaural thresholds are ~3 dB better than monaural (binaural summation), so mixing them across conditions confounds the comparison. A judge-proof project names which of these it controlled and how.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'A student wants to compare hearing thresholds "with and without background music." Version 1 (fatally flawed): they set laptop volume to 30%, play tones through earbuds in a normal room, and let the listener say when they hear each tone, experimenter watching. Every threat fires — no SPL calibration (volume 30% is not a level), room ambient ~40 dB(A) masking the tones, an unblinded experimenter cueing, earbuds with an unknown frequency response, and no catch trials. Version 2 (defensible): they calibrate each test frequency to a known dB SPL at the ear using a coupler + SPL meter checked with a 94 dB acoustic calibrator, run in the quietest room available with ambient measured and reported, use a 2AFC "which interval" task with randomized signal interval and a blinded script (so neither party cues the answer), include catch trials to bound false alarms, counterbalance the music/no-music order, and hold presentation strictly binaural throughout. Only Version 2 can claim its dB numbers mean anything or that the music — not an artifact — moved the threshold.',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges attack calibration and validity first because they are where nearly every amateur listening study fails — "I set the volume to 50%" is an instant tell that there is no real level axis, so the thresholds are uninterpretable. A student who can say "I calibrated each frequency to dB SPL at the ear with a coupler and a 94 dB calibrator, measured the room\'s ambient noise, blinded the operator, used catch trials, and kept presentation binaural" has pre-empted the exact objections a judge is trained to raise, turning a suspect demo into a credible experiment.',
