                    answerId: 'b',
                    explanations: {
                      a: 'Only at 1 kHz do phons and dB SPL coincide; at other frequencies the physical level differs from the phon value by the amount the ear is more or less sensitive there.',
                      b: 'Correct — the phon is defined by a loudness match to a 1 kHz reference tone, so 60 phons means "equally loud as 1 kHz at 60 dB SPL," regardless of the tone\'s own frequency.',
                      c: 'Phons are a logarithmic (dB-anchored) scale, so 60 phons is not perceptually "twice" 30 phons — that linear loudness role belongs to sones, not phons.',
                      d: 'The phon has nothing to do with a frequency ratio; it is a loudness-level unit.',
                    },
