                      b: 'Correct — sound is a mechanical wave that requires a material medium, so in vacuum it cannot propagate at all and an ultrasonic gauge is useless; radar uses electromagnetic microwaves, which travel through vacuum, so radar level sensing is chosen for vacuum/low-pressure vessels (as well as heavy-vapour and foam conditions).',
                      c: 'Optical transparency is irrelevant to ultrasound, which reflects off the acoustic-impedance change at the surface regardless of how the liquid looks.',
                      d: 'A metal tank is a normal, common installation; ultrasound reflects off the internal liquid surface fine — the tank material is not the failure condition.',
